      <section>
        <div class="num">03</div>
        <h2>One-pager</h2>

        <h3>What I understood</h3>
        <p>A Bangkok developer wants to know what a plot can hold <em>before</em> paying an architect: how big, how tall, what it might
        look like. The binding numbers are FAR (floor area over land), OSR (open space over floor area, not over land), setbacks and,
        in a few zones only, height.</p>
        <p>From the B.E. 2556 regulation: พ.3 is FAR 7.0, OSR 4.5 %, no height cap; ย.4 is 3.0 / 10 %; only ย.1 and ย.2 carry a hard
        12 m height. The finding that shaped the tool: <strong>in commercial zones the plan caps floor area, not height.</strong> Height
        falls out of plot ÷ floor plate. So the tool shows a FAR readout instead of a height slider.</p>

        <h3>What I did, and why</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>One zone, พ.3, done properly, with the full table exposed so the choice is visible rather than hidden.</li>
          <li>Any plot shape: setbacks are applied per edge on the real polygon, not to a bounding box.</li>
          <li>Two schemes from one envelope — tower-on-podium and stepped terraces — so the comparison is about plate strategy, not
          about rule interpretation.</li>
          <li>Whole floors only, never over entitlement: a scheme can land a few percent under target, shown as a note, rather than
          breaching FAR to hit a round number.</li>
          <li>The real site from OpenStreetMap: 201 buildings, 100 roads with kerbs and lane paint, a few hundred trees.</li>
          <li>The interface is deliberately not a generic dashboard. I studied Silicon Bricks' own Aayra viewer and built in its
          language: the render fills the screen, the panels float over it as warm paper glass, and the numbers sit in one pill at
          the bottom. A feasibility tool is a sales tool as much as a calculator.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Technical decisions, and why</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Astro, fully static.</strong> The chrome is HTML; the only client JS is the scene. Nothing to keep alive, nothing to time out.</li>
          <li><strong>Three.js, no tile server.</strong> OSM footprints are baked to one JSON at build time, so the app never depends on
          Overpass at runtime.</li>
          <li><strong>Hand-written geometry</strong> — shoelace area, per-edge miter offset with a health check, convex hull, rotating-calipers
          minimum-area rectangle for the tower plate. No turf or clipper payload, and every step is inspectable.</li>
          <li><strong>One model, three outputs.</strong> The same rules object drives the mesh, the compliance table and the isometric
          figure, so the picture cannot drift from the numbers.</li>
          <li><strong>Height-independent setbacks</strong>, because a setback that reacts to the height that reacts to the setback never
          settles — it oscillated by 12 % when it did.</li>
          <li><strong>Smoothness:</strong> merged context geometry, instanced trees and fins, shadows only from the proposal, pixel ratio
          capped at 2.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>What's still wrong, and what I'd do next</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The zone is user-selected. Next: wire the BMA zoning layer so the plot reads its own zone and sub-area.</li>
          <li>Setbacks outside the plan's own zones are modelled approximations. Next: per-district Building Control rules.</li>
          <li>Context heights are estimated where OSM is untagged. Next: LOD1 plus a height prior.</li>
          <li>Not modelled: prohibited uses, parking minimums, fire access, the FAR bonus, the draft 4th revision.</li>
          <li>No facade textures — it is a massing study, not a render.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <div class="num">04</div>
        <h2>Transcripts</h2>
        <p>Three raw sessions in order: connecting to the MCP server and starting the assignment; the zoning research; the build
        planning. Not summaries, not the coding sessions.</p>
        <div class="links">
          <a href="/transcripts/01.html">Session 01 — connecting to the MCP server, starting the clock, the brief</a>
          <a href="/transcripts/02.html">Session 02 — research, planning, the build, the redesigns</a>
        </div>
        <p class="fine">Both exported with <code>opencode session export</code>. Unedited except credentials redacted and long tool
        output truncated; every tool call can be opened in place.</p>

        <h3>What the AI missed, and how I caught it</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Its first setback model ate 57 % of the plot — an 11 m front setback plus 6 m sides on a 1,000 m² site — and produced a
          45-storey needle. The tell was the floor count against the plate area, not the height itself.</li>
          <li>It tied setback depth to the modelled height, which fed back into the height: GFA dropped when I switched from condominium
          to office. Caught by changing program and watching a number that should not move.</li>
          <li>The terraced scheme exceeded FAR by 12 % while the checklist still said pass, because the floor-rounding loop allowed one
          overshoot. Made the loop never exceed and let the plate land under target instead.</li>
          <li>The entitlement slider was wired to nothing. It moved, and changed nothing.</li>
          <li>On mobile the panels were still children of the 3D stage, so they were clipped and the page went blank below the tabs.</li>
          <li>Two earlier visual directions — a dark console, then a cool grey dashboard — did not match the warm paper language of
          Silicon Bricks' own Aayra viewer. The AI kept inventing a look instead of looking at the company; I had to paste the real
          product in front of it. That is the failure I learned the most from: it will happily produce a generic answer about your
          own product without ever opening it.</li>
          <li>Once pointed at Aayra, it copied the surface but missed the structure: floating panels over a full-bleed render, one
          dock, everything else hidden. The first attempt still had side rails eating a third of the screen.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <div class="num">05</div>
        <h2>Setup</h2>
        <h3>1. Tools, models and agents day to day</h3>
        <p>OpenCode, Codex and Hermes as the daily agent harnesses; Claude and DeepSeek-class models for long builds; Mistral, Qwen and
        Gemma hosted locally for inference work; Harbor for provider evaluations; vLLM and SGLang for serving.</p>
        <h3>2. How I run them</h3>
        <p>Parallel sessions per workstream, a rules file per repo, MCP servers for anything with a real API (GitHub, browsers, this
        assignment's own server), and small scripts where a tool does not exist.</p>
        <h3>3. Spend</h3>
        <p>Roughly <strong>$60&ndash;80 a month</strong>, mostly usage rather than subscriptions. My OpenCode dashboard for the last
        period: <strong>3.43 billion tokens</strong> across <strong>13,663 requests</strong> and <strong>$62.23</strong>, almost all of
        it DeepSeek V4.1 Flash and GLM 5.3. On top of that, about $10 for OpenCode's open-weight models and $20 on Claude or Gemini
        when a new model is worth testing. GPU rental is occasional, for hosting and evaluation runs.</p>
        <p class="fine">This assignment itself: 1.88M input and 155k output tokens, $0.57 of inference, measured in the session export.</p>
        <h3>4. Most ambitious thing built mostly with AI</h3>
        <p>AgentHire — <a href="https://aihirings.pages.dev">aihirings.pages.dev</a> — an agent-native hiring portal where candidates
        apply through their own agent over MCP. It broke on auth: the agent needed to manage profiles, upload r&eacute;sum&eacute;s and
        submit without a human in the loop, and the first OAuth flow assumed a browser.</p>
        <h3>5. Getting up to speed on an unfamiliar codebase or domain</h3>
        <p>Read the entry point and the data model first, run it before reading further, then let the agent map call sites while I keep
        the mental model. For a domain, I go to the primary document — here the ministerial regulation itself rather than summaries.</p>
        <h3>6. 3D, vision, diffusion, local or open-source models</h3>
        <p>Local and open-source: hosted Qwen and Gemma on an RTX 5090 and an H100 behind OpenAI-compatible APIs, full BF16 on the H100
        and GGUF on the consumer card, with continuous batching and KV-cache reuse. Vision and diffusion: limited — this project is my
        first real 3D build, done in Three.js.</p>
      </section>

